Side finding from #159, pre-existing and independent of it.

A non-ASCII prompt makes Perl emit

    Wide character in setenv at .../Foundation/Runner.pm line 58

on foundation's stderr. It fires at the KARR_REPO/KARR_ROLE/PROMPT
assignments, i.e. it has been there as long as the env has been set, and
#159 neither causes nor worsens it -- the bytes that reach the child are
correct UTF-8 either way.

But the house rule (CLAUDE.md) is that App::karr::Encoding owns every
crossing between characters and octets, and %ENV is one it does not own.
The warning is the symptom; the rule is the finding.

Two things to settle:

- where the crossing belongs. Encoding.pm already owns argv, std handles,
  ref blobs, YAML and JSON; %ENV is the same kind of edge and should be
  named there rather than handled ad hoc at the assignment.
- whether anything READS a non-ASCII env var back in. The write side is
  what warns; the read side is where a missing decode would corrupt.

Low priority: no wrong output today, only a warning on stderr and a hole
in a rule that is otherwise complete.
